WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.320
Shazam's boy-turned-super-dude Billy Batson
is a much-needed bolt of bright, cheerful

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:08.940
lightning for the normally dark-and-brooding
DCEU.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:13.299
In addition to being a great movie, the film
was also filled with nerdy Easter eggs for

00:00:13.299 --> 00:00:15.100
longtime comic book fans.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.539
Here are some of the small details we noticed
in Shazam!

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:19.540
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:25.000
While Shazam may be synonymous with DC Comics
today, the Big Red Cheese was originally published

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.560
by Fawcett Comics, beginning in 1939.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.390
Back then, he was known as Captain Marvel...

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.040
"No it's not!

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:33.240
That's not my name!"

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:35.150
...but that's a whole other story.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:40.110
His 1939 birthday makes him just slightly
younger than Superman, but those few months

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.110
would have a huge impact.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:47.540
In 1941, DC launched a campaign against Fawcett
to get them to stop publishing Captain Marvel,

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.910
alleging that he was just a copy of Superman.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:55.320
During the prolonged legal battle, which lasted
until 1953, DC even creatively "borrowed"

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.540
some story aspects from Captain Marvel, including
his power to fly, rather than just leap tall

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.040
buildings, and his bald-headed arch-nemesis.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.150
The court decided in favor of DC, an outcome
that crippled Fawcett.

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:12.010
Later, in 1972, DC bought the rights to a
number of Fawcett characters that they were

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:15.020
no longer allowed to use, including Captain
Marvel.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:19.920
But there seems to be no lingering hate between
DC and what was once Fawcett Comics, as DC

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.930
drops a pretty bold tribute to Shazam's true
origins.

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:27.170
Plastered across the lunchroom wall is the
name of Billy's alma mater: Fawcett Central.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:30.630
That wasn't the last name drop that the movie
had in store, though.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:34.870
When Billy's foster family finds the real
names of Billy's parents, his father is simply

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:36.530
identified as "C.C.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:40.180
Batson", a very clear reference to Shazam's
original co-creator, C.C.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:41.400
Beck.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:46.220
Shazam fans probably recognized Mr. Mind imprisoned
in a glass dome as Sivana entered the Rock

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:50.960
of Eternity, even before the film's mid-credits
scene gave the little worm a full reveal.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:56.450
That character is called Mr. Mind, and he's
a squirmy little worm with a very weird backstory.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:00.470
He's from outer space, he's friends with Nazis,
and runs a Monster Society.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:02.990
Sometimes, he can even eat dimensions.

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:07.490
After Sivana returns to the Rock of Eternity
as an adult and defeats the Wizard Shazam,

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:12.540
he not only lets the seven deadly enemies
of man escape, but Mr. Mind also breaks out…

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.380
setting up the weirdest mid-credits scene
of all time.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.040
Young Billy Batson is separated from his teenage
mother after she fails to win him a stuffed

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.540
tiger at a carnival.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.600
Later on, Shazam hands a tiger doll to a terrified
young girl to calm her.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.690
There are tigers on Shazam's costume.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:29.840
So, what's up with all these tigers?

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.860
As always, there's a reason rooted in comics:
one of Billy's best friends is a talking tiger.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:35.800
Obviously.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:42.900
That tiger, Mr. Tawky Tawny, first appeared
in 1947's Captain Marvel Adventures #79, where

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.099
it was revealed that he's just an ordinary
tiger who really, really wanted to wear clothes

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:50.290
and eat ice cream after consuming a brain-energizing
serum.

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:52.570
Why does he need his fancy brain potion?

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:55.840
To defend himself against a murder he'd been
accused of, of course!

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.520
It's all... weird.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:02.300
Tawny hops on a boat and shows up in the city,
where everyone is terrified of free-roaming

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.569
tigers, even when they politely ask questions.

00:03:04.569 --> 00:03:09.630
All is resolved, however, when Captain Marvel
secures Tawny a job as a museum guide.

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:13.489
All's well that ends well, and Tawny is given
a place among the Marvel family.

00:03:13.489 --> 00:03:17.770
If we already have talking worms, talking
tigers can't be that far behind.

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:19.280
And speaking of talking animals....

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:23.340
Somewhere on the Rock of Eternity, there's
a giant cavern full of doors to different

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:24.350
worlds.

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:28.390
One door in particular opens up to crocodiles
playing poker, and they aren't just an invention

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:29.390
for the movie.

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:33.631
The Crocodile Men from the planet Punkus first
showed up in the early days of Captain Marvel

00:03:33.631 --> 00:03:38.760
Adventures, and have names like Sylvester,
Herkimer, and Jorrk, working alongside Mr.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.130
Mind and his Monster Society of Evil.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:46.810
Much later, in 2006, a talking croc named
Sobek shows up, and a few weeks later longtime

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:50.990
Shazam villain Black Adam tears him apart
and makes boots out of his skin.

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:55.490
So, no matter how you look at it, those card
shark crocs were actually kind of a big deal…

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:57.100
and they'll probably be back for more.

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:02.040
Even though Dr. Sivana and Mr. Mind are some
of Shazam's most memorable foes, there's one

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:04.379
that's even more notable: Black Adam.

00:04:04.379 --> 00:04:09.090
DC announced Black Adam's solo movie in January
of 2017, and it was even supposed to show

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.550
up before Shazam! — so what gives?

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:15.530
The Wizard Shazam mentions in the film that
he had a previous champion who'd betrayed

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:18.340
him, and there's little doubt that he's referring
to Black Adam.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:22.490
In the original comic books, Black Adam is
given the powers of the six ancient gods,

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:27.039
but uses them selfishly, so the Wizard banishes
Black Adam to outer space.

00:04:27.039 --> 00:04:32.409
Adam spends 5000 years trying to make it back
to Earth, and when he does, he jealously terrorizes

00:04:32.409 --> 00:04:34.520
Billy Batson for many more decades.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:38.509
So, even though we're set up for a Mr. Mind
/ Sivana team up in Shazam!

00:04:38.509 --> 00:04:42.540
2, it's a good bet that the Wizard's old champion
will be showing up for a fight, too.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:47.100
Shazam! has been described as "Superman meets
Big", and that comparison couldn't possibly

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:51.169
be more appropriate, especially after seeing
one specific scene in the movie.

00:04:51.169 --> 00:04:56.229
While Tom Hanks and Robert Loggia may enjoy
a charming duet in the middle of FAO Schwartz

00:04:56.229 --> 00:05:00.689
on a king-sized floor piano in Big, Shazam!
gives its own spin on the scene during the

00:05:00.689 --> 00:05:03.169
shopping mall battle between Shazam and Sivana.

00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:07.419
While they don't quite make the same beautiful
music together, the colossal piano they wind

00:05:07.419 --> 00:05:09.659
up fighting on drives the connection home.

00:05:09.659 --> 00:05:15.289
And if you're looking for truly vintage references,
look no further than the iconic cover of 1945's

00:05:15.289 --> 00:05:20.919
Superman #32, where Superman shrugs off lightning
bolts with a casual "It tickles!"

00:05:20.919 --> 00:05:25.099
Similar words are spoken by Shazam while bullets
bounce off of him, really bringing home that

00:05:25.099 --> 00:05:28.620
this guy is as powerful as the Big Blue Boy
Scout.

